@h1 Paragraphs & prose h1@

Prose is the ground floor of Wit. You write plain text, and a blank line starts
a new paragraph. That is the whole rule to get going.

@@@pre
The rain had not stopped for three days.

Mara pressed her palm to the cold glass and counted
the boats that were no longer there.
pre@@@

Two blocks separated by a blank line become two paragraphs. A single line break
inside a block is just a soft wrap — the sentence continues.

@h2 Nothing is hijacked h2@

Wit never guesses structure from how a line begins. A line that starts with a
number, a dash, or a greater-than sign stays exactly what you typed:

@@@pre
1. Not a numbered list — just a sentence that starts with one.
> Not a block quote — just a line that opens with a bracket.
pre@@@

Your punctuation is yours. You reach for structure explicitly — with a
@a |href ../../start/nodes/| node a@ — only where you want it.
